em the confidence to bet, allowing me to extract more chips.\n2.  **Bet (100)**: This acts as a "value bet". It would get called by a weaker Jack or a mid-pocket pair (44-99). However, it folds out all the "air" (hands with nothing) which is likely what the opponent has.\n3.  **All-in**: This is a mistake. It forces a fold from everything except sets (22, 33, JJ), which beat me.\n\nReason: I have a monster hand (Top Two Pair) on a board that is unlikely to change the nuts on the Turn. The opponent has shown weakness by limping and checking. Betting now likely just wins the small existing pot by forcing a fold. Checking is the superior play to trap the opponent, inducing bluffs or allowing them to make a second-best hand on the Turn, thereby maximizing the potential chip gain to secure my lead.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
